From 186b0820e3413fdbfa9030e54b99329e9066f73d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <noreply@invalid>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 15:21:06 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] vulkan/wsi: Horizon, a resized window is suboptimal, not out
 of date

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 6 item 4 (Horizon WSI backend)
Why: a display mode change - docking the console, which takes the layer
 to 1920x1080 - was reported as VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR at the next
 acquire, and that result promises something this backend does not do.
 The specification's words are "further presentation requests using the
 swapchain will fail". They do not fail.
 .
 Nothing on the producer's side of the BufferQueue changes when the
 consumer resizes. The dequeue asks for NWindow::width/height, which
 this backend set at registration and nobody has touched, so every
 preallocated slot still matches and bqDequeueBuffer keeps handing them
 over; bqQueueBuffer validates the crop against the *buffer* rather than
 against the layer, so it keeps succeeding; and the layer's scaling mode
 is what puts the smaller image on the larger display. The frames still
 arrive, scaled rather than native - a swapchain that no longer matches
 the surface exactly and still presents, which is the definition of
 VK_SUBOPTIMAL_KHR.
 .
 It was wrong in both directions. An application that recreates on
 OUT_OF_DATE lost every frame between the mode change and the new
 swapchain; one that ignores the result - legal for SUBOPTIMAL and not
 for OUT_OF_DATE - stalled outright, because the acquire refused to hand
 over an image it could have handed over.
 .
 AND THE RECOVERY WAS A LOOP WITH NO EXIT ON THE DEFAULT PATH, which is
 the second half of this patch. NWindow::default_* is where libnx keeps
 the consumer's answer, and it maintains it in _nwindowUpdate from every
 nwindowQueueBuffer. The zero-copy path drives the producer directly
 through bqQueueBuffer - deliberately, for the reasons in this file's
 opening comment - so libnx never saw the queue's output and that field
 stayed at whatever bqConnect reported at start-up. wsi_horizon_get_
 extent reads exactly that field, so VkSurfaceCapabilitiesKHR::
 currentExtent would have kept reporting the pre-dock size: an
 application told its swapchain was suboptimal would have asked the
 surface what to build instead, been handed the size it already had, and
 built the same suboptimal swapchain again. The copy fallback never had
 it, because framebufferEnd goes through nwindowQueueBuffer.
 .
 Everything else this backend refuses stays VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR,
 because in each of those cases the next present really cannot happen:
 another swapchain has taken the window, the queue answered something
 that is not a retry, the compositor handed back a slot we already hold,
 the copy fallback has no framebuffer, or the application holds every
 image and is blocking for one only it can free. The full table is in
 docs/wsi.md 5.1 and in the comment above wsi_horizon_extent_changed().
Evidence: hardware, run 21 (2026-08-10, build 2026-08-10T13:44:43.834Z
 13336c0-dirty mesa:c5e9e66, game mode 3155 MiB), tests/t_vk_suboptimal.c:
 RESULT PASS (273/273), docs/hw-logs/t_vk_suboptimal-run21-PASS.log.
 .
 AND THE BOUNDARY OF THAT PASS, because it is not the whole claim. With
 the console undocked and nothing resizing the layer, a backend that
 never returns VK_SUBOPTIMAL_KHR and one that returns it only on a
 resize are indistinguishable, so sections A, B, C and E would have
 passed without this patch. VK_SUBOPTIMAL_KHR HAS NEVER BEEN RETURNED ON
 HARDWARE. What run 21 does establish is the rest of the contract:
 .
 - 2303 frames on both present paths with the rule checked in both
   directions per frame - SUBOPTIMAL exactly when the surface's
   currentExtent differs from the swapchain's imageExtent - and ZERO
   disagreements. Section D alone is 1803 of those frames, 30 s of
   continuous presenting with the acquire and the present each asserted
   every frame. That is the no-false-positives half, and it is the half
   a wrong implementation fails.
 - The two results stay distinct: on both paths a superseded swapchain
   answers VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR from acquire AND present while the
   surface's extent is checked to be unchanged, so that result cannot be
   coming from the resize check; an acquire that cannot be satisfied
   answers VK_TIMEOUT after three images were held.
 - Eight recreation generations alternating present path, 20 of 20
   frames each, then a swapchain created after all of them were
   destroyed; "no slot was left with the compositor" and "the driver
   tore down every object it created".
 .
 The binary was checked rather than assumed, because a stale driver has
 cost this project a run before: both wsi_horizon_swapchain_queue_present
 and wsi_horizon_swapchain_acquire_next_image in the shipped
 t_vk_suboptimal.elf build 0x3B9ACDEB (1000001003, VK_SUBOPTIMAL_KHR)
 into w0 with a mov/movk pair and return it.
 .
 The build id names mesa:c5e9e66 and this commit is not that hash. They
 are the same tree: c5e9e66 is this commit before the evidence above was
 written into its message, and amending a message changes the hash and
 nothing else. Same relationship patch 0071 records between mesa:597ea0a
 and mesa:85638f8 for run 20, and it is stated rather than left for a
 reader to find that `git log` does not contain the hash the artefact
 names.
 .
 The rest is the specification and the code paths, stated so a reader
 can check them.
 .
 The specification, on the two results: VK_SUBOPTIMAL_KHR is "a
 swapchain no longer matches the surface properties exactly, but can
 still be used to present to the surface successfully";
 VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR is "a surface has changed in such a way that
 it is no longer compatible with the swapchain, and further presentation
 requests using the swapchain will fail". VK_SUBOPTIMAL_KHR is a success
 code: the image is acquired and *pImageIndex is valid, which is why the
 check runs after the acquire here and only when one came back.
 .
 Mesa already carries it from both hooks and needed no change:
 wsi_common.c:1966 admits VK_SUBOPTIMAL_KHR beside VK_SUCCESS before
 marking the image acquired and signalling the caller's semaphore and
 fence, and wsi_common.c:2549 does the same after queue_present, with
 the final result taken as the first non-VK_SUCCESS one.
 .
 The stale-field half is read out of libnx's compiled library rather
 than assumed. bqQueueBuffer's signature settles one direction on its
 own - "Result bqQueueBuffer(Binder *b, s32 buf, const BqBufferInput
 *input, BqBufferOutput *output)" (buffer_producer.h:44) takes a Binder
 and no NWindow, so it structurally cannot maintain NWindow fields.
 The other direction is in native_window.o, disassembled from
 libnx.a: after "bl bqQueueBuffer" nwindowQueueBuffer stores -1 to
 [x19, #64] (cur_slot), copies eight bytes of the QueueBufferOutput
 from the stack to [x19, #112] as one d-register store, and sets
 [x19, #126] from numPendingBuffers > 1. Against the NWindow layout in
 native_window.h those offsets are cur_slot, the default_width /
 default_height pair, and consumer_running_behind. So libnx maintains
 exactly the two fields this patch writes, in exactly the place it
 writes them, and only on the path this backend does not take.
 .
 Why the resize cannot be provoked in software, which is why there is no
 hardware number here: the consumer's default buffer size belongs to VI.
 An IGraphicBufferProducer can read it - bqQueueBuffer returns it in
 BqBufferOutput, which is where this file gets it - and has no call that
 sets it. viSetLayerSize is a manager service a homebrew does not have,
 and libnx keeps the default window's ViLayer private in any case.
 .
 The build: aarch64-none-elf-gcc from the pinned toolchain image,
 scripts/build-mesa-nvk.sh, clean tree, -Wall -Wextra -Werror, no
 diagnostics.
Upstream: no - this file is the Horizon backend itself.
---
 src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c | 157 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 139 insertions(+), 18 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
index 2f41ef6..484571f 100644
--- a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
+++ b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
@@ -347,9 +347,11 @@ wsi_horizon_surface_get_capabilities(VkIcdSurfaceBase *surface,
    /* A fixed extent, and it is not a simplification: every buffer
     * registered on an NWindow must have the window's dimensions, and
     * nwindowSetDimensions refuses to change them while any buffer is
-    * registered. An application that wants a different size has to
-    * change the window, which this backend then reports as
-    * VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR at the next acquire. */
+    * registered. When the *consumer* changes it — docking the console —
+    * this stops matching the live swapchain's extent, which is reported
+    * as VK_SUBOPTIMAL_KHR and not as an error: see
+    * wsi_horizon_extent_changed for why that swapchain keeps
+    * presenting. */
    caps->currentExtent = extent;
    caps->minImageExtent = extent;
    caps->maxImageExtent = extent;
@@ -577,8 +579,8 @@ struct wsi_horizon_swapchain {
 
    VkExtent2D extent;
    /* The size the consumer last said this window is. Updated from every
-    * queue; a change is what turns the next acquire into
-    * VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR. */
+    * queue; a difference from `extent` is what makes this swapchain
+    * suboptimal — see wsi_horizon_extent_changed. */
    VkExtent2D reported;
    const struct wsi_horizon_format *format;
    uint32_t swap_interval;
@@ -1195,9 +1197,56 @@ wsi_horizon_release_slots(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain)
 
 /* ---- acquire ----------------------------------------------------- */
 
-/* True when the compositor has changed the window's size under the
- * swapchain. Checked at every acquire, which is where Vulkan expects to
- * hear about it.
+/* THE ONE CONDITION THAT IS SUBOPTIMAL RATHER THAN OUT OF DATE.
+ *
+ * Vulkan draws the line by whether the swapchain can still present:
+ *
+ *   VK_SUBOPTIMAL_KHR         the swapchain no longer matches the
+ *                             surface's properties exactly and can
+ *                             still be used to present successfully;
+ *   VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR  the surface has changed so that the
+ *                             swapchain is no longer compatible with
+ *                             it, and further presentation requests
+ *                             *will fail*.
+ *
+ * On this backend exactly one condition lands on the first side, and it
+ * is this one: the VI consumer reporting a layer size other than the
+ * one this swapchain registered its buffers at. Docking the console —
+ * which takes the display to 1920x1080 — is what produces it.
+ *
+ * WHY THAT KEEPS PRESENTING, WHICH IS THE WHOLE ARGUMENT. Nothing about
+ * the producer's side of the BufferQueue changes when the consumer
+ * resizes. The dequeue asks for NWindow::width/height, which this
+ * backend set at registration and nobody has touched, so every
+ * preallocated slot still matches and bqDequeueBuffer keeps handing
+ * them over; bqQueueBuffer validates the crop against the buffer and
+ * not against the layer, so it keeps succeeding; and the layer's
+ * scaling mode is what puts the smaller image on the larger display.
+ * The frames still arrive — scaled rather than native, which is
+ * precisely "usable but not matching".
+ *
+ * So this used to be VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR at the next acquire, and
+ * that was wrong in both directions. It promised that presentation
+ * would fail, when it does not; and it refused the application an image
+ * it could have rendered and presented, so an application that recreates
+ * on OUT_OF_DATE lost every frame between the mode change and the new
+ * swapchain, while one that ignores the result — which is legal for
+ * SUBOPTIMAL and not for OUT_OF_DATE — stalled outright.
+ *
+ * IT DEGRADES INTO THE OTHER RESULT ON ITS OWN. If the resize ever does
+ * make the queue refuse a buffer, that refusal is reported where it
+ * happens: bqQueueBuffer failing is VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR in
+ * wsi_horizon_present_zero_copy, and a dequeue failing for anything but
+ * "come back later" is the same in the acquire. Nothing here has to
+ * predict which it will be.
+ *
+ * EVERYTHING ELSE THIS BACKEND REFUSES STAYS OUT OF DATE, because in
+ * every one of those cases the next present really cannot happen:
+ * another swapchain has taken the window (`presentable` is false), the
+ * queue answered something that is not a retry, the compositor handed
+ * back a slot we already hold, the copy fallback has no framebuffer or
+ * no mapping, or the application holds every image and is blocking for
+ * one that only it can free.
  *
  * `reported` is the consumer's own answer, not ours: every queue comes
  * back with the size the BufferQueue believes the window is, and this
@@ -1205,6 +1254,15 @@ wsi_horizon_release_slots(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain)
  * instead would compare the swapchain's extent with the value this
  * backend itself wrote into the window at creation, which can never
  * differ — a resize check that cannot fire.
+ *
+ * NO STATE AND NO STICKY FLAG. This is a comparison of two values that
+ * are both current, so it answers "suboptimal *now*". It stays true for
+ * as long as the console stays docked, which is what makes the result
+ * repeat on every acquire and every present until the application
+ * recreates; and it stops being true by itself if the console is
+ * undocked again, which is correct — the swapchain matches the surface
+ * once more, and saying otherwise would send an application off to
+ * recreate a swapchain that was already right.
  */
 static bool
 wsi_horizon_extent_changed(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain)
@@ -1543,13 +1601,28 @@ wsi_horizon_swapchain_acquire_next_image(struct wsi_swapchain *wsi_chain,
       return VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR;
    }
 
-   if (wsi_horizon_extent_changed(chain))
-      return VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR;
+   const VkResult result =
+      chain->zero_copy
+         ? wsi_horizon_acquire_zero_copy(chain, info->timeout, image_index)
+         : wsi_horizon_acquire_fallback(chain, info->timeout, image_index);
 
-   if (chain->zero_copy)
-      return wsi_horizon_acquire_zero_copy(chain, info->timeout, image_index);
+   /* SUBOPTIMAL ONLY WHERE AN IMAGE ACTUALLY CAME BACK, and that is why
+    * the check moved below the acquire instead of staying above it.
+    *
+    * VK_SUBOPTIMAL_KHR is a success code: the specification says the
+    * image was acquired and *pImageIndex is valid, and
+    * wsi_common_acquire_next_image2 duly marks the image acquired and
+    * signals the caller's semaphore and fence on it. Returning it in
+    * place of an acquire would hand the application an index it never
+    * got and a semaphore nothing will ever signal.
+    *
+    * So VK_TIMEOUT, VK_NOT_READY and every error go back untouched —
+    * none of them acquired anything, and a caller that asked with a
+    * zero timeout still gets the answer it asked for. */
+   if (result == VK_SUCCESS && wsi_horizon_extent_changed(chain))
+      return VK_SUBOPTIMAL_KHR;
 
-   return wsi_horizon_acquire_fallback(chain, info->timeout, image_index);
+   return result;
 }
 
 static VkResult
@@ -1687,11 +1760,41 @@ wsi_horizon_present_zero_copy(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain,
    /* The size the consumer believes this window is, straight from the
     * queue that just happened. It is the only place a producer hears
     * about a display mode change — docking the console makes it
-    * 1920x1080 — and the next acquire turns a change into
-    * VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR. */
+    * 1920x1080 — and a change makes this swapchain suboptimal from this
+    * present onwards (wsi_horizon_extent_changed). */
    if (out.width != 0 && out.height != 0) {
       chain->reported.width = out.width;
       chain->reported.height = out.height;
+
+      /* AND LIBNX'S OWN COPY OF IT, because this path is the reason it
+       * would otherwise be wrong.
+       *
+       * NWindow::default_* is where libnx keeps the consumer's answer,
+       * and it maintains it in _nwindowUpdate from every
+       * nwindowQueueBuffer. This path does not go through
+       * nwindowQueueBuffer — it drives the producer directly, for the
+       * reasons in this file's opening comment — so after a display
+       * mode change libnx's copy stayed at whatever bqConnect said at
+       * start-up, forever.
+       *
+       * That is not cosmetic. wsi_horizon_get_extent reads exactly this
+       * field, so VkSurfaceCapabilitiesKHR::currentExtent kept
+       * reporting the pre-dock size: an application told its swapchain
+       * was suboptimal would have asked the surface what to build
+       * instead, been handed the size it already had, and recreated the
+       * same suboptimal swapchain — a loop with no exit, on the path
+       * that is the default on working hardware. The copy fallback
+       * never had the problem, because framebufferEnd goes through
+       * nwindowQueueBuffer and libnx updates the field itself.
+       *
+       * Two aligned u32s, written where libnx writes them and read
+       * where libnx reads them. It is the same unsynchronised pair
+       * vkGetPhysicalDeviceSurfaceCapabilitiesKHR already reads beside
+       * a concurrent present on the fallback path (docs/wsi.md § 7),
+       * so this adds no hazard that was not already there and is not
+       * this backend's to fix: the structure belongs to libnx. */
+      chain->window->default_width = out.width;
+      chain->window->default_height = out.height;
    }
 
    return VK_SUCCESS;
@@ -1798,10 +1901,28 @@ wsi_horizon_swapchain_queue_present(struct wsi_swapchain *wsi_chain,
       return VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR;
    }
 
-   if (chain->zero_copy)
-      return wsi_horizon_present_zero_copy(chain, image_index);
+   const VkResult result = chain->zero_copy
+      ? wsi_horizon_present_zero_copy(chain, image_index)
+      : wsi_horizon_present_fallback(chain, image_index);
 
-   return wsi_horizon_present_fallback(chain, image_index);
+   /* AND THIS IS WHERE THE NEWS ARRIVES, so it is where the application
+    * hears it first.
+    *
+    * The present that just happened is the call that reads the
+    * consumer's size — BqBufferOutput on the zero-copy path, the
+    * NWindow's own default_* on the fallback — so a mode change becomes
+    * visible to this backend inside the two functions above and nowhere
+    * else. Checking here reports it one frame earlier than the next
+    * acquire would, on the very present that learned about it, and
+    * VK_SUBOPTIMAL_KHR is the result Vulkan has for a present that
+    * succeeded against a surface the swapchain no longer matches.
+    *
+    * Only when the present succeeded: a failure is the more specific
+    * answer and must not be overwritten by this one. */
+   if (result == VK_SUCCESS && wsi_horizon_extent_changed(chain))
+      return VK_SUBOPTIMAL_KHR;
+
+   return result;
 }
 
 static VkResult
-- 
2.55.0.windows.2

